{\pard\qc\sa60\b\fs40\cf1 LIEUTENANT FIZZ\par}
{\pard\qc\sa240\b\fs26 Episode 1: The Cocoa Caper \emdash  for MS-DOS\par}
{\pard\sa200 This disk holds the DOS port of Lieutenant Fizz, Episode 1. The game's own simulation, written in Rust, runs as a 32-bit DOS program in VGA mode 13h (320 x 200, 256 colours) with AdLib music and effects, or PC-speaker effects.\par}
{\pard\sa200 It is built for a 486 with an x87 maths unit, and needs a VGA and at least 2 MB of free memory; with less than 10 MB the DOS extender pages to disk, which is slower. It has been run in DOSBox only: nothing has been tried on real hardware yet.\par}
{\pard\sb200\sa80\b\fs28\cf1 Run it\par}
{\pard\sa80 From the disk:\par}
{\pard\li360\sa80\f1 A:\line FIZZ\par}
{\pard\sa80 From a hard disk, copy everything on this disk to a folder and run FIZZ.EXE there.\par}
{\pard\sa80 FIZZ.EXE starts with a system check (what it found and a speed test); Enter goes on to the opening titles (any key skips them) and the title menu. If the machine lacks a VGA, an x87 or enough memory, the check says so and Esc leaves. The sound is set on the Options screen, which offers music and the effects' chip only with an AdLib. CWSDPMI.EXE, the DOS extender, must stay beside FIZZ.EXE.\par}
{\pard\li360\sa200\f1\fs20 FIZZ [level|map] [nopreview] [adlib|speaker|nosound]\line      [fps30|fps60] [lowdetail|highdetail]\par}
{\pard\sa200 FIZZ 3 starts in level 3 and FIZZ map starts on the overworld.\par}
{\pard\sb200\sa80\b\fs28\cf1 Keys\par}
{\pard\tx2600\sa40\b Arrows or WASD\b0\tab Move (Up and Down also climb and look)\par}
{\pard\tx2600\sa40\b Ctrl or Z\b0\tab Jump\par}
{\pard\tx2600\sa40\b Alt or X\b0\tab Pogo stick\par}
{\pard\tx2600\sa40\b Space or C\b0\tab Fire\par}
{\pard\tx2600\sa40\b Enter\b0\tab Confirm: enter a level marker, dismiss a card\par}
{\pard\tx2600\sa40\b Esc\b0\tab Pause menu, and back in a menu\par}
{\pard\tx2600\sa40\b Backspace\b0\tab Back to the overworld map\par}
{\pard\tx2600\sa40\b PgUp / PgDn\b0\tab Previous or next level\par}
{\pard\tx2600\sa200\b R, N, M, E\b0\tab Restart the level; night mode; music; effects\par}
{\pard\sb200\sa80\b\fs28\cf1 Saves and options\par}
{\pard\sa200 Saves are AUTO.SAV (written each time you reach the map), QUICK.SAV (F5 saves, F9 loads) and SLOT1.SAV to SLOT4.SAV (Pause, Save game). Options are in FIZZ.CFG. All of them are written beside FIZZ.EXE, so the disk must not be write-protected.\par}
{\pard\sb200\sa80\b\fs28\cf1 Sound test\par}
{\pard\sa200 TONETEST plays an A (440 Hz) on the AdLib and then on the PC speaker.\par}
{\pard\sb200\sa80\b\fs28\cf1 What is in it\par}
{\pard\sa200 Episode 1 from start to finish: the opening titles, New game with its opening story (Esc skips it), the overworld, every level and boss, and the ending and credits. The titles play again if the title menu sits untouched for a few minutes. STATUS.md in the source has what has and has not been checked.\par}
